<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:59:07Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Each side feels it 'could get a decisive military victory' in Sudan, Trump's Africa envoy says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:62f98e5d02a43ad69b7f104fe21a60f205f56e3e4362b86e7a938ac05da2f055</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:58:04Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T12:54:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20260925-each-side-feels-it-could-get-a-decisive-military-victory-in-sudan-trump-s-africa-envoy-says--9207dc16119d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260925-each-side-feels-it-could-get-a-decisive-military-victory-in-sudan-trump-s-africa-envoy-says" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24 on the sidelines of the UN General Assembly, Trump's Africa envoy Massad Boulos said that each side in Sudan feels it "could get a decisive military victory". Asked about Western Sahara, he acknowledged there is no "imminent solution that we can feel or touch". He also rejected Rwanda's accusations of bias over US sanctions, saying they "can easily be reversed".</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: what's driving the renewed violence in Tigray?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:243ae525985c414c6342002d05f00706e5a3e9249bd2c5d76d7ea6539bb2eed3</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:58:27Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:15:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-what-s-driving-the-renewed-violence-in-tigray--2c6abb0159c4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-what-s-driving-the-renewed-violence-in-tigray" /><summary type="html">Tigray's rebel authorities say they are now in a "full-blown war" with the Ethiopian government, as fighting spreads into the neighbouring Amhara and Afar regions. Ethiopia's federal army says 272 rebels were killed and 260 wounded in the north, while FRANCE 24 spoke to Jervin Naidoo, Political Analyst at Oxford Economics Africa, to assess the seriousness of the escalation and explain what is driving the renewed violence in northern Ethiopia</summary></entry><entry><title>Senegal: power cuts return as Dakar swelters in the heat</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d41fb577f5bbdac4d70369d93677b6ce73ba42734e6df0c93c7be12f653e0d0d</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:58:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:14:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/senegal-power-cuts-return-as-dakar-swelters-in-the-heat--fd18df6442db.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-power-cuts-return-as-dakar-swelters-in-the-heat" /><summary type="html">After years of largely stabilising its electricity supply, Senegal is once again facing power cuts, with outages returning to Dakar in September. The blackouts are fuelling frustration among residents as the capital endures a spell of intense heat.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: Tigray rebels claim "full-blown war"</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f24cb2520013bf1afd15dce94dd1a6bdecdb8a1d2659f74b8b9ab82a474e4fe8</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:58:33Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:13:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tigray-rebels-claim-full-blown-war--9b074c24592a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tigray-rebels-claim-full-blown-war" /><summary type="html">The Tigray People's Liberation Front says it is in a "full-blown war" with Ethiopia's federal government after seizing key airports and launching attacks into the neighbouring Amhara and Afar regions. The escalation comes days after the TPLF joined six other armed groups in a new alliance against the government, raising fears of a return to the devastating 2020-2022 conflict that killed at least 600,000 people.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: renewed Tigray fighting raises fears of return to civil war</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3ee388ce9bca3ea082cdab2e4acf254a8ec33e87083e7aa690a8cb61eb70e55f</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:58:36Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:12:34Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-renewed-tigray-fighting-raises-fears-of-return-to-civil-war--a8794692057f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-renewed-tigray-fighting-raises-fears-of-return-to-civil-war" /><summary type="html">Fears are mounting that Ethiopia could be sliding back towards civil war as fighting intensifies in the north, with the army saying it killed 272 TPLF fighters in Amhara while the rebels describe the conflict as a "full-blown war." The escalation comes after the TPLF joined a new alliance with six other armed groups. FRANCE 24's Tom Canetti has the latest from the ground.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tigray rebels in 'full-blown' war with government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:08db7e225ef1c97582b09808d58817432e19b95abfaf99593b138edc2e881de8</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:59:07Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T20:23:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-in-full-blown-war-with-government--26a3e13754ca.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-in-full-blown-war-with-government" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Tigrayans in northern Ethiopia start panic-buying supplies in the rush to stock up amid fears that months of tensions have finally tipped over into full-blown conflict. Also, power cuts return to Senegal. Plus Yemi Alade, the Nigerian superstar, is out with a new album and speaks to FRANCE 24.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>A look back at Tunisia's summer of shortages</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:79211f7b21b577c02369265c37fc9dc6e8a1eb2ee714967676a1897ecb0e5086</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T09:43:31Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260924-look-back-tunisiassummer-shortages--1433d0807877.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260924-look-back-tunisiassummer-shortages" /><summary type="html">Repeated power and water outages, long queues for bottled water, and poultry farms destroyed by the heat: millions of Tunisians were affected by water and electricity shortages over the summer. Our Observers say this is the result of years of underinvestment. The water shortage eased in late August when authorities sent millions of bottles of water to stores, but many are concerned the same thing could happen next summer.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>Tigray rebels seize airports</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f7387177667b216a31fa8fbb3eb9824efcbd8f0ebe643d880f8039e9a04cfc90</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T20:27:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-seize-airports--6203b2cdd57b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-seize-airports" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: TPLF fighters take over the airport in Tigray amid escalating conflict with federal troops. But first, concerns about South Africa's high rates of crimes are brought even more into focus as 11 people are killed at a party. And in eastern DR Congo, residents of Bukavu are once again searching through the ruins of their homes after another deadly fire.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Morocco’s centre-right PAM leads in provisional election results</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c4c48b2c6f0d5f8f2a5cd1447b96809f9a3b06055d812c3d9d4c4a23293fb850</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T13:57:36Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-morocco-s-centre-right-pam-secures-most-seats-in-election-provisional-results--18ecdd8f84ad.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-morocco-s-centre-right-pam-secures-most-seats-in-election-provisional-results" /><summary type="html">Morocco’s Authenticity and Modernity Party (PAM) won the largest number of seats in the country’s parliamentary elections, according to provisional results announced by the Interior Ministry Thursday. PAM, which is part of the kingdom's ruling coalition, made an effort to woo young voters by recruiting the head of the Royal Moroccan Football Federation on the campaign trail.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Fresh drilling for a lost Egyptian queen?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a70e20b22ed237b9688a2b0a794578d64b18dd1329d7ddc5c474ce18912b31b9</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T10:31:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20260923-fresh-drilling-for-a-lost-egyptian-queen--96525c3c0aab.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20260923-fresh-drilling-for-a-lost-egyptian-queen" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Wednesday, September 23: The papers look ahead to US President Donald Trump hosting his Chinese counterpart Xi Jinping. In other news, a Haitian-born novelist, tipped to win France's prestigious Goncourt literary prize, has been struck by an AI scandal. Finally, could the remains of Nefertiti lie behind the burial chamber of Tutankhamun?</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>ICC convicts Central African Republic rebel leader of crimes against humanity</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a8ddf0c4c65615034e659bc13c86a1246304846a68be6d087f5758f78f2482df</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T10:27:34Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-icc-convicts-central-african-republic-rebel-leader-of-crimes-against-humanity--d6e9d0e23f7a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-icc-convicts-central-african-republic-rebel-leader-of-crimes-against-humanity" /><summary type="html">The International Criminal Court on Wednesday convicted former Central African Republic militia commander Mahamat Said Abdel Kani of torture and unlawful imprisonment. Said oversaw a detention centre in the capital Bangui infamous for its brutal conditions during the country's sectarian civil war.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Moroccans vote in parliamentary elections after migrant rush to Ceuta</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4c8163a19f30adc8421a56fd461ce045208b92c370a97f6da7cf9629a6a8731e</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T07:14:59Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-moroccans-vote-in-parliamentary-elections-after-migrant-rush-to-ceuta--6d3270907457.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-moroccans-vote-in-parliamentary-elections-after-migrant-rush-to-ceuta" /><summary type="html">Moroccan voters choose new parliament Wednesday in elections dominated by economic concerns after a mass migration surge to the Spanish enclave of Ceuta in July highlighted rising youth unemployment and cost of living concerns. Nearly 16 million Moroccans have registered to elect 395 members of the House of Representatives.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>‘I had never heard of Eswatini’: Deported by the US to a country that rejects them</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0d3792f0ca4188695504728f7968282d881309a35e74ff1809924412e49aca6e</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T06:56:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-never-heard-eswatini-deported-by-us-country-that-rejects-them-forbidden-stories--9a79002fea05.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-never-heard-eswatini-deported-by-us-country-that-rejects-them-forbidden-stories" /><summary type="html">Migrants previously convicted by US courts have been imprisoned in Africa’s last absolute monarchy, Eswatini, over the past several months. Since being deported from the US, they have found themselves in a country about which they know nothing, and where local residents look on these “criminal” arrivals with suspicion. Their abrupt journey to the small southern African kingdom stems from a secret deal struck by Trump administration as part of his programme of mass deportations investigated by “The Deportation Project”, undertaken by Forbidden Stories and a network of 72 journalists across 15 countries.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>US refusing to give Sudan's de facto leader visa for UN</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2a27d5b3da39cb6e81940d690826ab5b35a8af386936c4a1e1e46add5ff9cc04</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-22T20:13:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/us-refusing-to-give-sudan-s-de-facto-leader-visa-for-un--35682f8a2d46.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/us-refusing-to-give-sudan-s-de-facto-leader-visa-for-un" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: The United States is withholding a visa for Sudanese army chief Abdel Fattah al-Burhan ahead of his scheduled UN appearance. Also, Ghana is beefing up its drugs authority in the wake of major drug busts in France, Belgium and South Africa. Plus it’s back-to-school season in Ivory Coast.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Lake Chad, a region under threat from climate change</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7a970c9aea956e93184989ea884ddd30f8e1f226552ad00ac844ef3b70061d08</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-22T11:30:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20260922-climate-change-overfishing-lake-chad-sees-fish-stocks-dwindle--8bb3fa3f65e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20260922-climate-change-overfishing-lake-chad-sees-fish-stocks-dwindle" /><summary type="html">People living in Chad’s Lake Province are bearing the brunt of climate change. The region and the communities that have developed there have historically depended on this vast body of water. Fishing, first and foremost, but also farming, are the pillars of local livelihoods. Yet both are increasingly disrupted by erratic weather patterns: dwindling fish stocks, damaged farmland and a rising cost of living.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia's main armed groups form anti-government alliance</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9e846271fd900d708dfb66a96210899122e1cef17e6385611f0e7fbfd73bc658</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T21:16:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-s-main-armed-groups-form-anti-government-alliance--d7f1443afa0c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-s-main-armed-groups-form-anti-government-alliance" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: A coalition of opposition groups band together in the hope of ousting Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed. Also, we speak to the deputy chief of the UN's peacekeeping mission in DR Congo. And with just a few days to go before legislative elections, young Moroccans are torn between hope and scepticism ahead of the crucial vote.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Deportation project: 72 journalists investigate US treatment of migrants</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:efd92ac7015e413c3c07b618a1f123a81e39fdfa8279e7c961dce3a07cea359d</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T08:55:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260921-deportation-project-72-journalists-investigate-us-treatment-of-migrants-1--dea57e7ef702.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260921-deportation-project-72-journalists-investigate-us-treatment-of-migrants-1" /><summary type="html">Under secretive deals with countries across Africa and Latin America, the Trump administration has deported thousands of migrants to countries they had never previously visited. Of the 25,000 deportations documented by reporters, the vast majority were sent across the border to Mexico. One of the journalists who investigated the story, Sofía Álvarez Jurado, explains how these people are forced across the borders to Mexico and what happens to them once they arrive.</summary></entry><entry><title>Investigation: In Cameroon, the victims of Trump’s deportations face uncertain fates</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8cf4471f7146d4e6929ac8708d5c9442cfb7a1b5f22067b4f97f87e17f7fe011</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T05:00:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-waiting-for-us-to-crack-victims-of-trump-deportations-face-uncertain-fates-in-cameroon--49f56fccb149.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-waiting-for-us-to-crack-victims-of-trump-deportations-face-uncertain-fates-in-cameroon" /><summary type="html">Under often-secret deals struck with third countries across Africa and Latin America, US President Donald Trump has deported thousands of people who were hoping to build new lives in the US to countries they’ve never set foot in before. Working with Forbidden Stories, FRANCE 24 investigated the stories of several African migrants whose lives were thrown into chaos by their abrupt deportation from the US to a temporary shelter in Cameroon’s capital Yaoundé.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Deported to Africa: An international investigation into Trump’s migrant policy</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:91810cbb3b4675443da8f41956a919e2f309a09b2e6f9624384174e18b5fa8f5</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T05:00:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-deported-to-africa-an-international-investigation-into-trump-s-migrant-policy--a08a651759dd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-deported-to-africa-an-international-investigation-into-trump-s-migrant-policy" /><summary type="html">As part of a consortium of 26 media outlets coordinated by Forbidden Stories, FRANCE 24 and RFI spent seven months investigating the secret deals struck between the administration of US President Donald Trump and several African countries. These “third countries” have agreed to host undocumented migrants detained by ICE and deported from the United States.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nine women found dead near Johannesburg sends shockwaves through South Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d033af5b0794dfca0a33c0c30f0556a9c3041f2e21a700d60bcdb1ea03937073</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-20T10:00:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/nine-women-found-dead-near-johannesburg-sends-shockwaves-through-south-africa--c2cdec2e5a2d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/nine-women-found-dead-near-johannesburg-sends-shockwaves-through-south-africa" /><summary type="html">Vigils, group runs and moments of silence: thousands of protesters gathered across South Africa this weekend to pay tribute to the nine women found murdered in eastern Johannesburg. In a country that records more than 60 homicides a day, the nine murders sent shockwaves through the population. Anger is mounting, and calls are growing for the government to take action. Story by Caroline Baum.</summary></entry><entry><title>China is now Rwanda’s biggest export market – now comes the hard part</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a97caf1092acf8fd81ba6074069f86c523a3dd83f4a23b8ef63b2ba86a48c92c</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T13:30:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260919-china-is-now-rwanda-s-biggest-export-market-_-now-comes-the-hard-part--a374c5f30bd4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260919-china-is-now-rwanda-s-biggest-export-market-%E2%80%93-now-comes-the-hard-part" /><summary type="html">In the first quarter of 2026, China became the leading destination for Rwandan exports, accounting for 26% of the total, compared with less than 5% just one year earlier. A spectacular shift, driven by the removal of Chinese tariffs on almost all Rwandan products. But on the ground, access to this market of 1.4 billion consumers depends less on tariffs than on meeting sanitary standards. Report by Aurore Bayoud.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tunisia’s booming boat industry: From pleasure craft to luxury yachts</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7b2648b8696f4990748fc52c78ab311e207c13793492ff7415afb3336cadf060</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T12:40:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tunisia-s-booming-boat-industry-from-pleasure-craft-to-luxury-yachts--be09a0ac3726.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tunisia-s-booming-boat-industry-from-pleasure-craft-to-luxury-yachts" /><summary type="html">In Tunisia, the summer is ending, but the boating season is still in swing. The boating industry has been quietly growing, despite an overall sluggish economy. Since the sector was reorganized through a trade association in 2024, there have been numerous foreign factories set up and Tunisia has become a hub for boat construction – from pleasure crafts to sophisticated yachts. Report by Lilia Blaise and Fadil Aliriza.</summary></entry><entry><title>37 die in Nigerian custody: Reports blame overcrowding, government disputes claim</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1c44790c0f8d650d32b434a6d19572632b161eb321554609a651790ce59de853</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T11:26:22Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/37-die-in-nigerian-custody-reports-blame-overcrowding-government-disputes-claim--2875a91ef8ec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/37-die-in-nigerian-custody-reports-blame-overcrowding-government-disputes-claim" /><summary type="html">Authorities in north-central Nigeria are investigating the deaths of 37 people while they were in custody for illegal mining. Officials say the detainees died at a detention facility in Niger state, with the Nigerian Security and Civil Defence Corps pointing to a possible disease outbreak. However, some reports suggest overcrowding and poor ventilation may have contributed to the deaths. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent Sam Olukoya.</summary></entry><entry><title>Suspected alcohol poisoning in Nigeria's Ondo State kills at least 49 people</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4a4ee6820c5ab2d4b1dbf0c5fade716670e84e27aeeb495ed5e953299c10ff0c</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T11:19:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/suspected-alcohol-poisoning-in-nigeria-s-ondo-state-kills-at-least-49-people--5aaebcf6dccb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/suspected-alcohol-poisoning-in-nigeria-s-ondo-state-kills-at-least-49-people" /><summary type="html">In Nigeria's Ondo state, at least 49 people have died and 100 others injured after drinking alcohol suspected to contain methanol. Local authorities say 'Monkey Shoulder', a locally brewed herbal drink with high alcohol content is believed to have been the source of the poisoning, but investigations are still ongoing. State police have announced the arrest of 15 people in connection with the incident. Story by Clemence Waller.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ebola outbreak reaches new areas in Democratic Republic of Congo</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b8db0b2bd89d8b18cf70a07dee88ba51358789aa25562ca6170af4d4afc0dadb</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T09:59:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ebola-outbreak-reaches-new-areas-in-democratic-republic-of-congo--c0ca8e99988f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ebola-outbreak-reaches-new-areas-in-democratic-republic-of-congo" /><summary type="html">In the Democratic Republic of Congo, the government claims that the Ebola outbreak has changed course, with cases trending downwards in hotspots. But that interpretation is being met with pushback from experts and health responders, who say the outbreak remains as bad as ever. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent Emmet Livingstone.</summary></entry><entry><title>Uproar in Nigeria’s Niger state after 37 people die in custody</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:af2ab3be0e1c87821059cd32c55fc5e299c65ad661721dc3e71762a3fa6be8e2</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-18T20:52:51Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/uproar-in-niger-state-37-die-in-custody--9b3070eb9dd2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/uproar-in-niger-state-37-die-in-custody" /><summary type="html">In tonight’s programme: An investigation is underway into the death in custody of 37 people accused of illegal mining in Nigeria. Also, Tunisia is emerging as a major boat-building hub, serving both local buyers and international markets. And finally, we speak to the author of “The moon will not burn us” by Ricci Shryock.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>At least 37 suspected illegal miners die in custody in central Nigeria</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ed5c2cbfba934f414e23440a3a72fc2099a47019abaa081132b04a5629adb523</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-18T00:27:53Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260918-37-suspected-illegal-miners-die-in-custody-in-central-nigeria--1d616cfc5edd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260918-37-suspected-illegal-miners-die-in-custody-in-central-nigeria" /><summary type="html">Nigerian authorities launched an investigation Thursday after 37 suspected illegal miners died in a civil defence detention facility in central Niger state, with conflicting accounts blaming either a disease outbreak or overcrowding and poor ventilation.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Serial killer suspicions in South Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1578ce212ed93beba2c4b46f8eba683c5db5a50024b22b5cf21212a04032c408</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T20:25:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/serial-killer-suspicions-in-south-africa--a7d4c3810954.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/serial-killer-suspicions-in-south-africa" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: the body of a ninth woman is found in Johannesburg as South African police start investigating a potential femicidal serial killer. Also, Africans are excited about what good use of the technology could mean to growth, but many are also worried about what an AI free-for-all could mean for their lives and livelihoods. And Chad’s first music school officially opened its doors on Wednesday night in N’Djamena.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>DR Congo says Ebola outbreak has passed its peak</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:13ae96cfa9691f103c311f2caaf64dfaecab63f83d29a517fe41619596051850</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T13:36:09Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/dr-congo-says-ebola-outbreak-has-passed-its-peak--314bad08c0b7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/dr-congo-says-ebola-outbreak-has-passed-its-peak" /><summary type="html">More than 3,510 people have died from Ebola in eastern DR Congo since the outbreak was declared four months ago, with Ituri accounting for 78% of confirmed cases. Congolese authorities say the epidemic has now passed its peak, pointing to signs that the outbreak is beginning to slow.</summary></entry><entry><title>Zimbabwe farmers brace for El Niño by saving water</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9c445f814cde66f322fb40e1a9fa59314b907879feb6a8686fc7fb77432aceb0</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T13:33:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260917-zimbabwe-farmers-brace-for-el-ni_o-by-saving-water--f6a1044bac7d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260917-zimbabwe-farmers-brace-for-el-ni%C3%B1o-by-saving-water" /><summary type="html">Some communities in Zimbabwe are adopting water-saving techniques such as aquaponics and rainwater harvesting to protect crops as the country braces for El Niño-driven drought. Sharon Mazingaizo has this report.</summary></entry></feed>
